# Motorola SM8635 device file contexts.
#
# UFS BSG (SCSI generic) character devices.
#
# qseecomd's gpt_init_service enumerates the UFS LUNs and reads each disk's GPT
# header through /dev/<host>:<channel>:<target>:<lun>. On arcfox those nodes are
# 0:0:0:0 .. 0:0:0:5. QCOM's sepolicy_vndr labels only two of them --
#
#   /dev/0:0:0:49476   u:object_r:vendor_bsg_device:s0          (the WLUN)
#   /dev/0:0:0:4       u:object_r:vendor_ufs_lun4_bsg_device:s0
#
# -- so 0, 1, 2, 3 and 5 fall through to the generic u:object_r:device:s0 and
# every read is denied. The boot log shows exactly that asymmetry: LUN 4 reads
# fine ("Successfully read 512 bytes from dev /dev/0:0:0:4") while all the
# others fail with errno 13, qseecomd exits 255, and with no TEE the keymint
# HAL never registers IKeyMintDevice -- which is what stalls keystore2, then
# vold, then the whole boot.
#
# tee already holds `allow tee vendor_bsg_device:chr_file rw_file_perms` in
# sm8650/generic/vendor/common/tee.te, so labelling is all that is needed here.
# The literal /dev/0:0:0:4 entry in QCOM's file_contexts still wins over this
# regex -- in file_contexts an exact path beats a pattern regardless of order.
/dev/0:0:0:[0-9]+               u:object_r:vendor_bsg_device:s0


# The FPC fingerprint HAL had NO label, so init refused to start it -- while
# sm8635-common/manifest.xml DECLARES android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint.
# That is the same declared-but-absent trap that weaver just demonstrated: a
# system_server thread blocks in waitForDeclaredService forever. (It was not the
# thread that tripped the watchdog this time, but it is the next one in line.)
#
# Stock uses vendor_hal_fingerprint_fpc_exec, a Motorola type our policy does not
# define. hal_fingerprint_default_exec is AOSP's own type for an AIDL fingerprint
# HAL and does exist here, so use that; any extra access the FPC implementation
# needs will show up as denials rather than as a service that never runs.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.biometrics\.fingerprint-service\.fpc  u:object_r:hal_fingerprint_default_exec:s0

# See hal_sensorext.te: labelling the binary AND adding the service_contexts
# entry must happen together.
# Stock runs this binary in hal_graphics_composer_default, which already carries
# the /mnt/vendor/persist, display and sensor access it needs. Our bespoke
# vendor_hal_sensorext domain had only init_daemon_domain, and the service
# registered fine and then SIGABRTed on its first transaction inside
# SensorExt::initAlsComp reading /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors/als_* (84 crashes in
# 410s). No AVC was logged for vendor_hal_sensorext, so this is a stock-parity
# argument rather than a proven denial; if it still aborts, check whether those
# als_* files exist under /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors at all.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/motorola\.hardware\.sensorext-service  u:object_r:hal_graphics_composer_default_exec:s0

# MODEM EFS / FSG BLOCK DEVICES -- the RF calibration path.
#
# Every partition on this device fell through to the generic u:object_r:
# block_device:s0, so rmt_storage (which serves the modem's EFS over QMI) was
# denied its own storage:
#
#   avc: denied { read write } for comm="rmt_storage" name="sdd18"
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_rmt_storage:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:block_device:s0
#
# sdd18 is fsg. The consequence is not a log nuisance: with no EFS the modem has
# no RF calibration and REFUSES TO POWER THE RADIO ON, so there is no signal, no
# SIM, no SMS and no mobile data:
#
#   RILJ: RADIO_POWER error 71
#   CommandException: NO_RF_CALIBRATION_INFO
#   AnomalyReporter: "No RF calibration data"
#
# vendor_rmt_storage is already allowed rw on vendor_modem_efs_partition_device
# (sepolicy_vndr/legacy-um/generic/vendor/common/rmt_storage.te:51), so labelling
# is all that is needed. Paths and types taken verbatim from stock's
# /vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts for the 1d84000.ufshc controller.
#
# THE SLOT-SUFFIXED PARTITIONS TAKE A DIFFERENT TYPE. This is the trap: stock
# carries BOTH an unsuffixed `fsg` entry AND an `fsg_[ab]` entry, and they are
# NOT the same type --
#   fsg        vendor_modem_efs_partition_device   (raw EFS, served by rmt_storage)
#   fsg_[ab]   vendor_modem_block_device           (an ext4 filesystem Android mounts)
# Labelling fsg_a as modem_efs (which this file did on the first attempt) denies
# e2fsck, and the boot log shows fs_mgr find the filesystem unclean, unmount it
# for a check, and the check fail:
#   e2fsck: Permission denied while trying to open .../by-name/fsg_a
# The whole set below is taken verbatim from stock's own vendor_file_contexts for
# this controller rather than extrapolated -- extrapolating the suffix is exactly
# what got it wrong.
#
# modemst1, modemst2 and fsc are genuinely not slot-specific and keep the EFS
# type. Verified live: with these, rmt_storage's denial disappears and it serves
# /boot/modem_fs1 and /boot/modem_fs2 from a cold boot with zero denials.
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modemst1        u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modemst2        u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsc             u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsg             u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsg_[ab]        u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modem_[ab]      u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/bluetooth_[ab]  u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/ssd             u:object_r:vendor_ssd_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/persist         u:object_r:vendor_persist_block_device:s0


# MOTOROLA UTAG FACTORY DATA -- the ro.vendor.hw.* / ro.vendor.product.* family.
#
# /vendor/bin/init.oem.hw.sh is what loads the UTAG store and derives those
# properties. It was labelled vendor_file, so init refused to exec it at
# early-init:
#
#   Could not start service: File /vendor/bin/init.oem.hw.sh
#   (labeled "u:object_r:vendor_file:s0") has incorrect label or no domain
#   transition from u:r:init:s0 to another SELinux domain defined.
#
# and /proc/hw held nothing but `all` and `reload` all the way to boot_completed.
# Type and path are stock's (vendor_file_contexts line 84); see init_hw.te.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.oem\.hw\.sh                   u:object_r:init_hw_exec:s0

# The partitions behind that store. ueventd applies the by-name label to the
# underlying node, which is what the driver's blkdev_get_by_path() ends up
# checking -- see kernel.te for the kretprobe that proved the -EACCES. Types and
# paths are stock's (vendor_file_contexts lines 863, 878, 929).
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/hw               u:object_r:hw_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/utags            u:object_r:utags_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/utagsBackup      u:object_r:utags_block_device:s0


# ===========================================================================
# THE 15 MOTOROLA /vendor/bin SERVICES init REFUSED TO START.
#
# Every one of them failed identically to init.oem.hw.sh above:
#
#   Could not start service: File /vendor/bin/<name>
#   (labeled "u:object_r:vendor_file:s0") has incorrect label or no domain
#   transition from u:r:init:s0 to another SELinux domain defined.
#
# Types and path patterns are taken verbatim from stock's
# /vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts; the line number of each is quoted.
# Note stock is not internally consistent about the pattern -- some entries use
# the /(vendor|system/vendor)/ alternation and some a bare /vendor/ -- and that
# is reproduced rather than normalised, because the bare ones are the ones
# stock's own build verified.
# ===========================================================================

/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/batt_health                         u:object_r:batt_health_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/capsense_reset                      u:object_r:capsense_reset_exec:s0
/vendor/bin/copy_qc_cert\.sh                                    u:object_r:qclicense_copy_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hardware_revisions\.sh              u:object_r:hardware_rev_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.attest_model\.sh         u:object_r:mmi_attest_chk_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.block_perm\.sh           u:object_r:init_block_perm_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.boot\.sh                 u:object_r:mmi_boot_exec:s0
/vendor/bin/init\.mmi\.cap\.sh                                  u:object_r:init_capsensor_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.display\.sh              u:object_r:init_display_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.touch\.sh                u:object_r:init_touch_exec:s0
# ⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT LABELLED IN THIS CHANGE -- the domain in init_usb.te ships,
# but without this line init still cannot start the script, which is the point.
# init.mmi.usb.sh reconfigures the USB gadget, and this port's own
# init/init.arcfox-usb.rc restores a gadget config that init.mmi.usb.rc blanks --
# a workaround that was written and tuned while this script was NEVER RUNNING.
# Enabling both at once risks losing adb, which is the only channel to the device
# and the only way to diagnose everything else in this change. Uncomment it on its
# own, in its own boot cycle, with fastboot ready.
# /(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.usb\.sh                  u:object_r:init_usb_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.mmi\.wls\.sh                  u:object_r:init_wls_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/mbm_spy                             u:object_r:mbm_spy_exec:s0
/vendor/bin/mot_flip_count                                      u:object_r:mot_flip_count_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/pstore_annotate\.sh                 u:object_r:apanic_exec:s0

# apanic_exec is shared by FOUR scripts on stock, not just pstore_annotate.sh,
# and all four ship on this device (verified with `ls /vendor/bin` over adb).
# init.mmi.rc defines a service for each: vendor.pstore_annotate (:371),
# vendor.apanic_copy (:377), vendor.bl_logs_copy (:389, the same script with -i)
# and vendor.apanic_annotate (:409, which runs apanic_save.sh). Labelling only
# pstore_annotate.sh would leave the other three failing exactly as before, so
# the whole set is labelled together, as stock does.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/apanic_copy\.sh                     u:object_r:apanic_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/apanic_save\.sh                     u:object_r:apanic_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/apanic_annotate\.sh                 u:object_r:apanic_exec:s0

# NOT LABELLED: /vendor/bin/kpgather. Stock gives it a domain of its own and a
# typetransition from apanic (vendor_sepolicy.cil:11485). It is not ported here,
# so the `kpgather` that apanic_copy.sh runs stays in the apanic domain via
# `allow apanic vendor_file:file execute_no_trans` -- which is strictly less
# privilege separation than stock but is the alternative to a 16th domain whose
# only caller is already covered.


# The data directories these services own. Without their own types the domains
# would have to be given all of /data/vendor (u:object_r:vendor_data_file:s0,
# which is what these are labelled on the device today). See file.te.
/data/vendor/hardware_revisions(/.*)?     u:object_r:hwrev_data_file:s0
/data/vendor/dontpanic(/.*)?              u:object_r:panic_data_file:s0
/data/vendor/dropbox-add(/.*)?            u:object_r:vendor_dropbox_data_file:s0
/data/vendor/aplogd(/.*)?                 u:object_r:aplog_data_file:s0
/data/vendor/power_supply_logger(/.*)?    u:object_r:aplog_data_file:s0

# batt_health's persistent battery facts. /mnt/vendor/persist subdirectories do
# get their file_contexts labels applied -- verified live: /mnt/vendor/persist/data
# is already vendor_persist_data_file and /sensors is vendor_persist_sensors_file
# while the rest of the tree is mnt_vendor_file.
/mnt/vendor/persist/batt_health(/.*)?     u:object_r:persist_batt_file:s0

# NOT LABELLED: /mnt/vendor/persist/factory. Stock gives it persist_factory_file
# and grants mmi_boot read on it, but mmi_boot ALSO holds mnt_vendor_file
# dir/file/lnk read (which it needs anyway, for /mnt/vendor/persist/rkp_complete
# -- confirmed u:object_r:mnt_vendor_file:s0 on the device). So the only thing
# in /mnt/vendor/persist/factory that mmi_boot reads, the `fti` blob, is already
# reachable and a second type would add nothing.


# The three extra Motorola partitions. As with hw/utags above, ueventd applies
# the by-name label to the underlying device node, so the by-name path under the
# 1d84000.ufshc controller is what has to be listed. Verbatim from stock; note
# that stock's OTHER entries for these types are under the legacy
# /dev/block/bootdevice/by-name/ spelling and name partitions (hob, dhob, clogo,
# logs, logs-charging) that do not exist on arcfox -- those are deliberately not
# extrapolated here.
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/cid              u:object_r:cid_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/kpan             u:object_r:kpan_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/logfs            u:object_r:bllogs_block_device:s0


# FACE UNLOCK. The binary was labelled vendor_file, so init could not start it
# while the VINTF fragment already declared IFace/default -- see
# hal_face_default.te for the live proof and for why this uses AOSP's
# hal_face_default rather than stock's Motorola hal_biometric_face type.
#
# The legacy HIDL android.hardware.biometrics.face@1.0-service.face is
# deliberately left unlabelled: we ship the binary because it comes with the same
# blob set, but the AIDL HAL is what serves IFace/default and the HIDL one should
# never start.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.biometrics\.face-service\.face-isv  u:object_r:hal_face_default_exec:s0

# The two directories the face HAL's init rc creates in on post-fs-data. Paths and
# type are stock's (vendor_file_contexts:593, :859).
/data/vendor/\.face(/.*)?                       u:object_r:tetras_config_file:s0
/data/vendor/tetras_data/config(/.*)?               u:object_r:tetras_config_file:s0


# CAMERA CALIBRATION AND IMAGER DATA. These three labels only move directories
# off the generic mnt_vendor_file / vendor_data_file fallthroughs so that
# hal_camera_default can be granted them by name; the grants themselves, the
# captured denials, and an explicit statement of what has NOT been verified are
# all in hal_camera_default.te. Paths and types are stock's, verbatim from its
# vendor_file_contexts:763, :727 and :1005.
#
# Both /data paths already exist on the device (owner camera:camera) and are
# currently vendor_data_file, so init's post-fs-data restorecon picks up the new
# labels on the next boot after the file_contexts hash changes. There is NO
# type_transition here and stock has none either, so if /data is wiped and the
# HAL recreates the directories itself they come back as vendor_data_file and the
# denial returns -- stock has that same hole, do not mistake this for complete.
/mnt/vendor/persist/camera(/.*)?                u:object_r:persist_camera_file:s0
/data/vendor/misc/imager(/.*)?                  u:object_r:imager_data_file:s0
/data/vendor/misc/imager/metadata(/.*)?         u:object_r:imager_metadata_file:s0


# The FPC fingerprint HAL's state directory. Stock: vendor_file_contexts:637.
# See hal_fingerprint_default.te; the type_transition there covers the case
# where the HAL creates it before restorecon runs.
/data/vendor/\.fps(/.*)?                        u:object_r:fps_data_file:s0

# /dev/mmi_sys_temp -- Motorola's system-temperature character device, opened
# read/write by thermal-engine and falling through to u:object_r:device:s0:
#
#   avc: denied { read write } for comm="thermal-engine-" name="mmi_sys_temp"
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_thermal-engine:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:device:s0
#        tclass=chr_file
#
# Stock labels it thermal_device (vendor_file_contexts:1191). THAT TYPE IS NOT
# AVAILABLE HERE: it is declared only in sepolicy_vndr/legacy-um/legacy, and
# this board resolves to sepolicy_vndr/sm8650 (UM_6_1_FAMILY), which has no such
# type. Using it fails the build --
#   libsepol.context_from_record: type thermal_device is not defined
#   vendor_file_contexts: line 1074 has invalid context u:object_r:thermal_device:s0
# -- so a type of our own is declared in device.te and granted in
# vendor_thermal-engine.te. Same access, name local to this tree.
/dev/mmi_sys_temp                               u:object_r:vendor_mmi_thermal_device:s0

# NO ENTRY FOR /mnt/vendor/persist/audio HERE. QCOM's sepolicy_vndr already has
# one (sm8650/generic/vendor/common/file_contexts:458, identical to stock's
# vendor_file_contexts:734) and a second copy is a hard build error:
#   vendor_file_contexts: Multiple same specifications for
#   /mnt/vendor/persist/audio(/.*)?
# The denied file is NOT under that directory -- see hal_audio_default.te.

# rild's RF power-cutback state. Verified present on the live device and
# currently u:object_r:vendor_data_file:s0, which rild has no write on.
# Stock: vendor_file_contexts:740. See rild.te.
/data/vendor/misc/cutback(/.*)?                 u:object_r:cutback_data_file:s0

# storsec / prov / logo. Unlabelled, so the boot-control HAL is denied getattr
# on all three while it inventories the by-name directory. Types and paths are
# stock's (vendor_file_contexts:911, :912, :933). See hal_bootctl_default.te.
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/storsec_[ab]     u:object_r:vendor_custom_ab_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/prov_[ab]        u:object_r:vendor_custom_ab_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/logo_[ab]        u:object_r:vendor_custom_ab_block_device:s0

# The two Motorola wireless-charging HALs, labelled 2026-09-01 so init can start
# them (they carried the generic vendor_file and init refused the transition).
# Both lines are VERBATIM from stock's own
# vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts, escapes intact. Stock also labels
# @1.0-service and @2.0-service variants of each with the same exec types; those
# binaries do not exist in this ROM, so the entries for them are not copied --
# an unmatched file_contexts spec is dead weight, not parity.
# See hal_motpowershare_default.te / hal_motwlc_default.te.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/motorola\.hardware\.wireless\.powershare-service   u:object_r:hal_motpowershare_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/motorola\.hardware\.wireless\.wlc-service          u:object_r:hal_motwlc_default_exec:s0
